.Dd July 27, 2026
.Dt SECUREDKIM-CHECK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm securedkim-check
.Nd command-line DKIM signature verification tool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f Ar message-file
.Op Fl n Ar nameserver
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl b Ar bits
.Op Fl -max-key-records Ar count
.Op Fl m Ar ms
.Op Fl -refuse-l
.Op Fl -no-normalize
.Ar message-file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
verifies every DKIM-Signature header on an RFC 5322 message file and prints
the result of each as
.Dq key=value
lines on standard output.
It calls the same
.Fn verifySignature
.Xr securedkim 8
calls, with the same header list shape, so a result from
.Nm
is a statement about the shipped verifier rather than about a separate
implementation written to pass tests.
.Pp
Every signature is reported, not just the first to pass: RFC 8463's own
example message carries two independent signatures, and one valid signature
must not hide another's failure.
This is also what makes
.Nm
suitable for differential testing against other DKIM implementations.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl f Ar message-file
Message file to verify.
May also be given positionally.
Required.
.It Fl n Ar nameserver
DNS nameserver IP address.
Default:
.Dq 127.0.0.1 .
.It Fl p Ar port
DNS nameserver port.
Default: 53.
.It Fl b Ar bits
Minimum RSA key bits accepted from a signer's DNS key record.
Default: the RFC 8301 floor (1024).
Reconciled with that floor exactly as
.Xr securedkim 8
does: a value below 1024 is silently raised, so this flag cannot make
.Nm
accept a signature the shipped daemon would reject.
.It Fl -max-key-records Ar count
Key records to try at one selector before giving up.
Default: 3.
.It Fl m Ar ms
Wall-clock budget for the whole evaluation, in milliseconds.
Default: 20000, matching the daemon's
.Cm MaxEvaluationMs .
0 disables the limit.
.It Fl -refuse-l
Report
.Dq policy
for a signature carrying
.Cm l=
instead of honouring it.
RFC 6376 \[sc]8.2 sanctions either treatment; the daemon's default is to
honour
.Cm l= ,
matching this tool's default.
.It Fl -no-normalize
Do not rewrite a bare CR or LF in the file to CRLF while reading it.
.Nm
rewrites by default so that an
.Pa .eml
file edited on a Unix host, which is usually LF-only, can still be verified
under DKIM's CRLF canonicalization.
Use this flag when the file is already CRLF-canonical and a bare CR or LF is
body
.Em data
that canonicalization must not touch \(em the milter itself never applies
this rewrite, because it receives body octets verbatim over the milter
protocol.
.It Fl h
Display usage information.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT
.Bl -tag -width "sig.<n>.unsigned" -compact
.It Cm signatures
Count of DKIM-Signature fields found.
.It Cm sig.<n>.result
.Dq pass , Dq fail , Dq temperror , Dq permerror , Dq neutral , or Dq policy .
.It Cm sig.<n>.domain
The signature's
.Cm d=
tag.
.It Cm sig.<n>.selector
The signature's
.Cm s=
tag.
.It Cm sig.<n>.reason
Failure detail, when the verifier supplied one.
.It Cm sig.<n>.testing
Present with value
.Dq true
when the signing key carries
.Cm t=y .
.It Cm sig.<n>.unsigned
Octets of body the signature does not cover, when
.Cm l=
is honoured.
.It Cm result
.Dq pass
if any signature passed, else the first signature's result, else
.Dq none .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It 0
A verdict was reached and printed to standard output, including
.Dq fail .
.It 2
The tool could not run: the message file could not be read or parsed, an
option was invalid, or memory was exhausted.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Verify every signature on a message:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
securedkim-check message.eml
.Ed
.Pp
Verify against a conformance suite's own zone, treating
.Cm l=
as disqualifying:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
securedkim-check -n 127.0.0.1 -p 5353 --refuse-l message.eml
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr securedkim 8 ,
.Xr securedkim-sign 8 ,
.Xr securedkim-genkey 8 ,
.Xr securedkim-testkey 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%T DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Signatures
.%R RFC 6376
.Re
.Rs
.%T Use of RSA-SHA-256 and Ed25519-SHA-256 in DKIM
.%R RFC 8463
.Re
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Daniel Morante
